## How Tab Completions Work

### Generation Flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[User types code] --> B[Cursor analyzes context]
    B --> C[Collects context sources]
    C --> D[Current file at cursor]
    C --> E[Open editor tabs]
    C --> F[Recently edited files]
    C --> G[Project structure]
    
    D --> H[Send to Tab model]
    E --> H
    F --> H
    G --> H
    
    H --> I[Generate completion]
    I --> J[Display ghost text]
    J --> K{User action}
    K -->|Tab| L[Accept completion]
    K -->|Right Arrow| M[Accept next word]
    K -->|Escape| N[Dismiss]
    K -->|Continue typing| O[Generate new suggestion]
```

### Context Sources

The Tab completion model uses the following context sources:

| Source | Description | Influence |
|--------|-------------|-----------|
| **Current file** | Code at and around the cursor position | Primary context |
| **Open tabs** | Files currently open in the editor | High — provides related imports, types |
| **Recently edited** | Files modified in the last few minutes | Medium — recent patterns inform suggestions |
| **Project structure** | Directory layout and file names | Low — helps choose between similar names |
| **Cursor symbols** | Classes, functions, variables in scope | High — ensures correct typing |

### Model Selection for Tab

Tab uses a separate model from Chat and Agent modes, chosen for speed:

| Recommended Model | Strength | Trade-off |
|-------------------|----------|-----------|
| **Claude Haiku** | Fast, accurate, good for code | Less creative than Sonnet |
| **GPT-3.5 Turbo** | Fast, widely available | Lower quality on complex patterns |
| **Local models (7B-13B)** | No external API, fully private | Slower on local hardware, variable quality |

